Z||000005||Gold-86SE||12-28-2001||07:06 PM||||<BR>UPDATE!<P>I found the problems with the headlights.<P><BR>First the isolation relay was bad.<P>Second the actuator relay was bad.<P><BR>ok, some details.<BR>I finally had an idea to examine the stamp numbers on the relays to see if anything matched.  Viola, they did... and the actuators were not in the correct locations.<P>Found a good Isolation Relay and replaced the bad one, and the headlights went up.<P>The Actuator Relay on the driver side headlight I don't think is totally bad, just iffy, but I replaced it anyway.  It's symtoms were this: when the Isolation Relay was replaced the Actuator Relay on the driver side headlight operated correctly when I turned off the lights, but it acted like it still wanted to send the headlights down.  Simply put, I could hear an electronic tick of the motor turning in the down direction when the headlights were turned off.  I replaced the Actuator Relay and this problem went away.<P><BR>Clarification of Relays for those not familiar with the setup.<P><BR>Isolation Relay<BR>This is the relay that is mounted on the driver side fender well inside the driver side headlight bucket. It is the same size as the Actuator Relay, but has a completely different roll in the headlight operation.<P>The part number stamped on the Isolation Relay was: 10022620 yours may be different, so check your application.<P><BR>Actuator Relays<BR>These are located on the frame of the headlights.  On the driver side headlight, the Actuator Relay is located on the right side of the frame, and for the passenger side headlight, the right Actuator Relay is located on the left side of the frame.<P>The part number stamped on the Actuator Relay was this: 10027587 yours may be different, so check your application.<P>On 84-86 Fieros the Actuator Relays (unless replaced somewhere along the line) should have the same number and the Isolation Relay should be an off number.<P>This link provided me with the greatest help <A HREF="http://www.members.tripod.com/FieroDough/hedlight2.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.members.tripod.com/FieroDough/hedlight2.htm</A>  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||kiteman||12-29-2001||11:39 AM||||Glad to hear the link helped out.  It helped me the last time I worked on mine.<P>If one of your actuators ever strips the plastic gear, Rodney Dickman offers a repair kit and instructions for a little over twenty dollars.  Beats replacing the motor.  He also has an actuator rebuild kit (brushes etc).||||reg||10
Z||000007||kiteman||12-29-2001||11:40 AM||||Glad to hear the link helped out.  It helped me the last time I worked on mine.<P>If one of your actuators ever strips the plastic gear, Rodney Dickman offers a repair kit and instructions for a little over twenty dollars.  Beats replacing the motor.  He also has an actuator rebuild kit (brushes etc).||||reg||10
Z||000008||SLYGUY||12-29-2001||07:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR> Rodney Dickman offers a repair kit and instructions for a little over twenty dollars.  .[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The driver's side headlight on my 88GT flopped around like a fish and then quit altogether. The motor would run, but not raise the headlight. I found the "Bushing Rebuild Kit" at Rodney's. It was only $4 so I splurged and ordered bushing kits for both headlights. <P>$8 and 36 hours later the kits showed up. The instructions included were first rate. I took the assembly apart, cleared out the fine white powder (remains of previous bushings) and popped the little 'bushings' in. Look very similar to 1" glue sticks, but are obviously polypropelyne. <BR>20 mins. later and headlight rose,uhmm, to the occasion.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
